# Using rudof to compare schemas

```elixir
Mix.install(rudof_elixir: [path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)], kino: "== 0.19.0")
```

## Introduction

This document demonstrates comparing ShEx schemas with [rudof](https://rudof-project.github.io/). The setup cell installs the local Elixir wrapper and Kino; the comparison itself runs in the native library.

```elixir
alias Rudof.Session
import ExUnit.Assertions
rudof = Session.new!()
```

## Comparing Schemas

It is possible to use rudof to compare different schemas. For example:


```elixir
before = ~S"""
PREFIX : <http://example.org/>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
:Person { :name xsd:string ; :age xsd:integer ; :weight xsd:float ; :worksFor . }
"""

after_schema = ~S"""
PREFIX ex: <http://example.org/>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
ex:Person { ex:name xsd:string ; ex:birthDate xsd:date ; ex:worksFor . }
"""
```

> Elixir adaptation: Comparison selectors are absolute IRIs. The native result describes structural similarities and differences, not logical equivalence of schemas.


```elixir
native_options = [
  mode1: :shex,
  mode2: :shex,
  format1: :shexc,
  format2: :shexc,
  reader_mode: :lax,
  shape1: "http://example.org/Person",
  shape2: "http://example.org/Person",
  output_format: :json
]

{:ok, json} =
  Session.show_schema_comparison(rudof, before, after_schema, native_options)
diff = JSON.decode!(json)
assert Map.has_key?(diff["equal_properties"], "http://example.org/name")
assert Map.has_key?(diff["equal_properties"], "http://example.org/worksFor")
assert Map.has_key?(diff["properties1"], "http://example.org/age")
assert Map.has_key?(diff["properties1"], "http://example.org/weight")
assert Map.has_key?(diff["properties2"], "http://example.org/birthDate")
diff
```

```elixir
{:ok, text} =
  Session.compare_shex(rudof, before, after_schema,
    shape1: "http://example.org/Person",
    shape2: "http://example.org/Person",
    output_format: :internal
  )

IO.puts(text)
```

## Additional Elixir examples

These checks extend the original lesson using the wrapper’s native API.


```elixir
{:ok, json} =
  Rudof.compare_shex(before, before,
    shape1: "http://example.org/Person",
    shape2: "http://example.org/Person"
  )
same = JSON.decode!(json)
assert same["properties1"] == %{}
assert same["properties2"] == %{}
same
```

## Close the session

Release the native state when finished. To revisit earlier cells, evaluate from the beginning.


```elixir
:ok = Session.close(rudof)
```
